              "text": "Whether **ambrosia** is a food or a drink depends on whether you are talking about ancient mythology or modern cuisine:\n\n### 1. In Greek Mythology (Generally Food)\n* **Typically, it is Food:** In most classic Greek myths (such as the works of Homer), **ambrosia** is described as the **food** of the gods, while **nectar** is their **drink**. Consuming them gave the Olympian gods their immortality and eternal youth. \n* **The Exception:** The distinction wasn't always strictly set. A few ancient writers reversed the roles. For example, the poet Sappho famously described ambrosia as a drink (comparing it to a divine wine) and nectar as the food. \n\n### 2. In Modern Cuisine (Food)\n* **It is a Food:** Today, \"ambrosia\" refers to **ambrosia salad**, which is a popular American dessert salad. It is a sweet food typically made by mixing whipped cream (or Cool Whip) and sour cream with mandarin oranges, pineapple, shredded coconut, maraschino cherries, and mini marshmallows.",
